    "meal_prep": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "m_sub": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "Make it ahead",
            "subheading_size": "subtitle-md",
            "secondary_color": true
          }
        },
        "m_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What you can do early, and what you can't",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Chop the dill and mince the garlic hours ahead and keep them separate in the refrigerator &mdash; that is the fiddly part and it holds fine. Thawing is the other thing worth planning: overnight in the refrigerator is better than the cold-water shortcut if you have the time.</p><p>What does not stretch is the marinade. Thirty to sixty minutes is the window the recipe is written around. Baked, it is good cold the next day, flaked into a salad or onto toast with lemon &mdash; arguably a better second meal than most cooked salmon.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_btn": {
          "type": "button",
          "settings": {
