    "faq_dill": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_salt": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why is there no salt in the marinade?",
            "text": "<p>Because salt draws moisture out of the surface and starts firming the proteins, and over thirty to sixty minutes that is enough to change the texture. You would be part of the way to gravlax rather than to a soft bake. Season generously as the fish goes into the oven instead &mdash; it seasons the surface without having had time to pull water out.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when it's done?",
            "text": "<p>Go by the fish, not the clock. The thickest part should have just turned opaque and the flakes should separate under light pressure from a fork. Pull it there &mdash; it keeps cooking on the hot tray, and salmon that looks perfect in the oven is usually overdone by the time it reaches the table.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_broken": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why break it instead of serving whole fillets?",
            "text": "<p>Partly because it looks generous on a platter, and partly because the pieces catch the garlic, the pan juices and the lemon on more surfaces than an intact fillet does. It also makes portioning irrelevant, which is the point of putting it in the middle of the table.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_turmeric": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will the turmeric stain everything?",
            "text": "<p>It will mark a wooden board, a plastic spatula and your fingers, and it does come out of most fabric if you get to it quickly. Use a glass or ceramic dish rather than a light-coloured plastic one, and mix with a metal spoon. A pinch is all the recipe wants &mdash; more turmeric gives you a duller colour and a chalkier taste, not a better one.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_longer": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I marinate it longer than an hour?",
            "text": "<p>The recipe is written for thirty to sixty minutes and that is where it is at its best. There is no acid in this marinade, so a longer sit is far less punishing than it would be with lemon or vinegar in the bowl &mdash; but the garlic keeps getting stronger and the dill keeps going limp, so you gain nothing by stretching it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack is best, about 12 hours. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never thaw at room temperature, and never in warm water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_salt",
        "q_done",
        "q_broken",
        "q_turmeric",
        "q_longer",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
